</div>
</figure>
<figure id="S3.F1" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2510.09045v1/lct_diag.png" id="S3.F1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:471/251;" width="471" height="251" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 1: </span>An overview of the identifier extraction and replacement algorithm for long source code translation. The process involves identifier extraction, classification into syntactic categories, replacement using an identifier mapping strategy, code translation, and final restoration to ensure syntactic and semantic correctness.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this section, we outline the task description and provide a detailed explanation of our proposed approach as depicted in the Figure <a href="#S3.F1" title="Figure 1 ‣ 3 Methodology ‣ Cost-Efficient Long Code Translation using LLMs while Leveraging Identifier Replacements" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>, to perform long code translation with LLMs through the use of identifier replacement.</p>
</div>
<section id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph"><span id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Task Description</span>:</h4>

<div id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The task of translating source code in a zero-shot scenario with a LLM <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}</annotation></semantics></math> can be described as using a multinomial sampling strategy to convert code written in a source language <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{s}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{s}</annotation></semantics></math> into corresponding code in a target language <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Formally speaking, given an input code sequence <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{s}=\{x_{1},x_{2},\ldots,x_{n}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{s}=\{x_{1},x_{2},\ldots,x_{n}\}</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{s}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{s}</annotation></semantics></math>, the <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}</annotation></semantics></math> generates the output sequence <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{t}=\{y_{1},y_{2},\ldots,y_{m}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{t}=\{y_{1},y_{2},\ldots,y_{m}\}</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>, such that the functionality of <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{s}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{s}</annotation></semantics></math> is preserved.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">To mitigate context length limitations, we utilize an <span id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">identifier extraction and replacement</span> strategy during translation. First, we identify the set of unique long identifiers, <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{I}_{s}=\{i_{1},i_{2},\dots,i_{k}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℐ</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><msub><mi>i</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{I}_{s}=\{i_{1},i_{2},\dots,i_{k}\}</annotation></semantics></math>, within the source code <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{s}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{s}</annotation></semantics></math>. These identifiers are then replaced with a set of generalized placeholders, <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{P}=\{p_{1},p_{2},\ldots,p_{k}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{P}=\{p_{1},p_{2},\ldots,p_{k}\}</annotation></semantics></math>, resulting in a simplified and shorter source sequence <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C^{\prime}_{s}=\{x^{\prime}_{1},x^{\prime}_{2},\dots,x^{\prime}_{n}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>C</mi><mi>s</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mi>n</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C^{\prime}_{s}=\{x^{\prime}_{1},x^{\prime}_{2},\dots,x^{\prime}_{n}\}</annotation></semantics></math>. The LLM translates <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C^{\prime}_{s}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>C</mi><mi>s</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C^{\prime}_{s}</annotation></semantics></math> in the target language <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>. Finally, in a post-processing step, the placeholders identifiers <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{P}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{P}</annotation></semantics></math> are mapped back to corresponding
original identifiers <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{I}_{s}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℐ</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{I}_{s}</annotation></semantics></math> within the translated code <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>, preserving the code’s functionality and semantics.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph"><span id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Identifier Extraction and Replacement</span>:</h4>

<div id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We utilize tree-sitter <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">Brunsfeld and contributors ()</a></cite>, a robust parsing tool, to systematically extract relevant long identifiers from source code. By generating accurate syntax trees across various programming languages, tree-sitter enables us to identify key programming constructs, which are then considered as primary candidates<span id="footnote2" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">2</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">2</sup>
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">2</span>
              
              
              
            A detailed statistics of the probable identifier list has been reported in Table <a href="#S6.T4" title="Table 4 ‣ 6 Ablation Study ‣ Cost-Efficient Long Code Translation using LLMs while Leveraging Identifier Replacements" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a></span></span></span> for identifier replacements. Following are the key programming constructs serve as primary candidates for identifier extraction and replacement.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<ul id="S3.I1" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S3.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S3.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Function Definitions:</span> To capture function names and associated parameter identifiers.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S3.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Variable Declarations:</span> To extract variable names, ensuring the preservation of variable semantics across the translation process.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S3.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.I1.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Class and Method Declarations:</span> To capture class names and method signatures, crucial for maintaining object-oriented structure.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I1.i4" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S3.I1.i4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I1.i4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.I1.i4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Reserved Identifiers: </span> Identifiers such as <span id="S3.I1.i4.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">this</span>, <span id="S3.I1.i4.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">self</span>, <span id="S3.I1.i4.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">super</span>, <span id="S3.I1.i4.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">null</span>, <span id="S3.I1.i4.p1.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">true</span>, and <span id="S3.I1.i4.p1.1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">false</span> are not replaced to maintain language-specific semantics. Additionally, built-in function names such as <span id="S3.I1.i4.p1.1.8" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">print</span> (Python), <span id="S3.I1.i4.p1.1.9" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">System.out.println</span> (Java), <span id="S3.I1.i4.p1.1.10" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">std::cout</span> (C++), and keywords like <span id="S3.I1.i4.p1.1.11" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">def</span>, <span id="S3.I1.i4.p1.1.12" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">class</span>, <span id="S3.I1.i4.p1.1.13" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">static</span>, and <span id="S3.I1.i4.p1.1.14" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">return</span> are preserved to ensure syntactic correctness.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Employing these key programming constructs, we developed grammars for each programming language which are then used to extract the potential identifiers suitable for replacements.
Let <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{I}_{s}=\{i_{1},i_{2},\ldots,i_{k}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℐ</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><msub><mi>i</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{I}_{s}=\{i_{1},i_{2},\ldots,i_{k}\}</annotation></semantics></math> represent the set of extracted identifiers. For each <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i_{j}\in\mathcal{I}_{s}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>i</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℐ</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i_{j}\in\mathcal{I}_{s}</annotation></semantics></math>, we assign a unique placeholder <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{j}\in\mathcal{P}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{j}\in\mathcal{P}</annotation></semantics></math> of a small token length = <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2</annotation></semantics></math> and produce a simplified source code <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C^{\prime}_{s}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>C</mi><mi>s</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C^{\prime}_{s}</annotation></semantics></math> from original source code <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{s}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{s}</annotation></semantics></math>. This way, we achieve the length reduction, <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta l" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta l</annotation></semantics></math>, in the token length as given by:
<math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta l=\sum_{j=1}^{k}\left(|i_{j}|-|p_{j}|\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo rspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>k</mi></msubsup><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta l=\sum_{j=1}^{k}\left(|i_{j}|-|p_{j}|\right)</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|i_{j}|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>i</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|i_{j}|</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p3.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|p_{j}|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|p_{j}|</annotation></semantics></math> denote the token length of the original identifier and its corresponding placeholder, respectively.
After translation is complete and the LLM generates the output sequence <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p3.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C^{\prime}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>C</mi><mi>t</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C^{\prime}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> in the target language, a reverse process is applied to restore the original identifiers. Let <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p3.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math> represent the reverse mapping function, which takes translated sequence <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p3.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C^{\prime}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>C</mi><mi>t</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C^{\prime}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> and original set of identifiers <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p3.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{I}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℐ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{I}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> in the target language, and produces the final translated sequence <math id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p3.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>:</p>
<table id="S3.Ex1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{t}=g(C^{\prime}_{t},\mathcal{I}_{t})=\{y_{1},y_{2},\dots,y_{m}\}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>C</mi><mi>t</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℐ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{t}=g(C^{\prime}_{t},\mathcal{I}_{t})=\{y_{1},y_{2},\dots,y_{m}\}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<table id="S3.Ex2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\quad g:C^{\prime}_{t}\times\mathcal{I}_{t}\rightarrow C_{t}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>C</mi><mi>t</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℐ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\quad g:C^{\prime}_{t}\times\mathcal{I}_{t}\rightarrow C_{t}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p3.2" class="ltx_p">This post-processing step ensures that placeholders in the translated code are replaced with the corresponding identifiers from the target language, preserving the semantics of the original source code. This complete method is described in the Algorithm 1.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S4" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">4 </span>Experimental Setup</h2>

<figure id="S4.F2" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2510.09045v1/lct_inst.png" id="S4.F2.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:471/228;" width="471" height="228" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 2: </span>The figure illustrates the transformation of source code during the identifier replacement (IdRep) process.
<span id="S4.F2.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">(a)</span> represents the original source code with long, descriptive identifiers, while
<span id="S4.F2.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">(b)</span> shows the modified code where identifiers are replaced with shorter placeholders.
This transformation helps reduce token count, enabling LLMs to process longer sequences efficiently while preserving semantic correctness.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this section, we provide an overview of the dataset used in our experiments. We then explain various closed and open source LLMs, along with their specific configurations, to assess their capacity in translating long code sequences using identifier replacements.</p>
</div>
