<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 1: </span>Statistics of XcodeEval dataset. The table shows the total number of code instances across five programming languages (C, C++, Java, Go, Python) in both the Original and Reduced subsets.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S4.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To analyze our approach of code translation via identifier replacement, we partitioned the dataset into three subsets based on token length: those with lengths greater than or equal to 2000, 4000, and 8000 tokens. These lengths were determined using the TikToken tokenizer <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib25" title="" class="ltx_ref">Pan et al. (2024c)</a></cite>. We also filtered out instances that lacked corresponding test cases to ensure accurate evaluation. Table <a href="#S4.T1" title="Table 1 ‣ 4.1 Dataset ‣ 4 Experimental Setup ‣ Cost-Efficient Long Code Translation using LLMs while Leveraging Identifier Replacements" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> represents the total number of source code instances across various languages for both the original and reduced subsets. The “Original Dataset" provide raw counts prior to filtering, while the “Reduced Dataset" columns illustrate the instances that were retained.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.2 </span>LLM Models</h3>

<div id="S4.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this work, we use models such as GPT3.5-Turbo <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib35" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ye et al. (2023)</a></cite>, GPT4o-Mini <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hurst et al. (2024)</a></cite>, Mixtral (8*7B) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">Jiang et al. (2023)</a></cite><span id="footnote3" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">3</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">3</sup>
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">3</span>
              
              
              
            It is noteworthy that, while CodeLlama and Mixtral models are theoretically capable of handling inputs more than the mentioned tokens, they often produce noise beyond this limit.</span></span></span>, and CodeLlama <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">Grattafiori et al. (2023)</a></cite>. The context window of these models during inference are 16K, 128K, 8K and 4K respectively. The model choice is driven by their widespread use and popularity, as well as the constraints of computing capabilities.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
